  "interpretation": [
    "Slow regularized frictional slip is sufficient to cause the baseline retention loss in these loaded states. Timestep and Newton iteration convergence did not rule out this contact-model mechanism.",
    "Original three placements lose bilateral load at 4.3/3.9/4.9 seconds under baseline; image10mm loses at 12.3 seconds. NoSlip3 retains all four 30-second holds with at most 0.164 mm ball/tool drift.",
    "impratio100 reduces early slip about 8\u201310 times but misses the strict gate on one original placement: seed4 reaches 10.27 mm drift and fails at 29.5 seconds despite retaining bilateral force.",
    "The effective jaw/ball sliding friction coefficient is 1.0 from priority1 upstream gripper geoms, overriding ball priority0 nominal0.6. Scene friction sweeps do not change this gripper-ball coefficient.",
    "Some weak individual contacts approach the sliding bound. The observed image case has weighted sliding utilization0.221 and only0.61% normal load on nearly saturated contacts; reducing friction regularization permits long holds without increasing the coefficient. This is mechanism evidence, not a full grasp-wrench certificate."
  ],
  "recommendation": [
    "Keep existing experiment inputs and default contact physics fixed. An explicit opt-in numerical-contact sensitivity path is useful, labeled as uncalibrated and excluded from historical results.",
    "Prefer impratio sensitivity within the convex elliptic solver; retain NoSlip as a diagnostic comparator. NoSlip removes friction regularization in a postprocessing pass, makes inverse dynamics ill-defined and can destabilize complex multicontact scenes. A 30-second hold alone does not justify default adoption.",
    "Set options before reset/acquisition in an independent followup to verify that success is not conditional on a nominal-physics loaded state. Compare to a real stationary loaded hold before claiming physical contact agreement."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Privileged GT acquisition and stationary holds, not learned task-completion trials.",
    "Three original-scene placements plus one fixed image-derived hypothesis, not four independent physical scenes.",
    "No physical validation of jaw envelopes, stiffness, friction, servo load/current response or support height."
  ]
}
